    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Four things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are reconstruction.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. The exact slider positions on the valve panel.</b> The panel is clearly five vertical sliders plus a horizontal one, but the handle heights in the footage were read three different ways across passes — one pass put them high/low/middle/low/high, another put them all at the bottom. At this resolution the handles are a few pixels tall and the panel is dark, so this page describes the mechanism and points you at the paper clue rather than printing a height for each slider.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. The lever order for the pipe network.</b> We can see red levers, flanged pipe segments and a green indicator, and the recording clearly works the puzzle in two sittings. What we cannot honestly reconstruct is the winning sequence — the clip is cut and sped up, and a partial order would be worse than no order.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. Where the skeleton key comes from.</b> The key is in the inventory at 4:39 and is used at the gate a second later, but the pickup itself happens off-camera between our sampled frames. This page therefore does not claim a source for it — only that it is the item that finishes the level.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. The reward and the coin count.</b> The <b>LEVEL COMPLETED!</b> card is in our copy, which is more than most rooms in this chapter gave us, but it carries only the title and two rule lines. There is no coin figure, no star rating and no "next" button on the frame our clip ends on, so no reward number appears on this page. The coin balance in the top bar reads 3230 throughout the run, before and after the card.
    </div>
